          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "**Please contact a doctor, urgent care, or your diabetes team today. If you feel worse, call emergency services.** A reading over 20 that lasts all day, with you feeling sick, needs same-day medical assessment.\n\nThe General clinical reference \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus\" says that very high glucose with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, deep or rapid breathing, drowsiness, or confusion may indicate a hyperglycemic (very high blood sugar) emergency that needs immediate medical attention. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department now if you have any of these.\n\nA symptom check in this tool returned a \"routine\" rating, but it also said that is not a clearance. Treat the guidance above as the more important signal.\n\nThe \"Diabetic emergencies\" guidance notes that illness raises blood sugar, and that diabetes medication should not simply be stopped because someone is not eating. Your own clinician should give you a sick-day plan. I can't give you doses or medication changes.\n\nThese sources don't cover your specific medicines, your target numbers, or what your readings mean for you. Your clinician or pharmacist can answer those.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
